					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/novaedearx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1310897); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1310897&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607374164" alt="NovaeDeArx" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1310897)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/novaedearx" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1310897); return false;" >NovaeDeArx</a></span> <span class="odate time_1440525612 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Aug 2015 18:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2364029">
									

<p>Wow, you really ran with the criticism! I'm impressed on the turnaround time, to say the least.</p>
<p>Let's see&#8230; On the positive side, I feel as if the logs tell a much more cohesive story, and that story has much more of a &quot;creepy&quot; vibe to it than previous. Huge improvement all around, honestly. I like the idea of the Commercial Apocalypse a lot, too - I didn't expect that and it's the first taste of &quot;creepy&quot; in the article.</p>
<p>As for things that don't work as well as they could or areas for possible improvement:</p>
<ul>
<li>The log progression might work better if &quot;Alternate Property Logs&quot; went before &quot;Alternate Media Log&quot;. Then it's Sopranos Universe -&gt; Sopranos &amp; Other Stuff -&gt; Other Stuff before we get into the recursive testing.</li>
<li>A bunch of people get twitchy when [DATA EXPUNGED] gets tacked on the end of stuff. It's generally agreed to work best as a &quot;Monster in the shadows&quot;, where you can kinda make out what it is but it's creepier because you can't quite see the shape of it. It's actually more work to use it correctly than not, simply because you have to set up an expectation or creeping suspicion in the reader's mind and then use the redaction to loosely cover it up with a tablecloth, letting them decide whether they want to uncover it or not (by thinking about it too long or looking for the hints you scattered around). If you just use it as &quot;Oh there's something scary behind that black box, be scared!&quot;, it's ineffective and probably counterproductive. You've got about three stuck on the ends of logs that don't work very well; either reduce it to just the last one or try to give the reader more information to creep them out.</li>
<li>The recursive inception-style tests were a neat idea, but it needs a little more spit and polish. Through the third recursion, it all works and there's a nice progression of weird -&gt; weirder -&gt; uh oh, but then the next-to-last is a little disappointing (I'd strike it entirely and try moving the &quot;static&quot; test into here, saying that all media was now tuned to static in the <em>Snakebite</em> world, maybe all reflective surfaces as well?). I'm not sure about the final one, but don't just end it on an expungement, or justify it if you do. Perhaps have the camera come out of the hallway into the restaurant at the finale of the Sopranos right before everything usually goes black, but instead the researchers see [DATA EXPUNGED FOR COGNITOHAZARD]. Cleanup in Aisle 3, the new researchers are barred from doing this, and the old researchers are implied to not be around anymore.</li>
<li>There's an odd spelling error or two and some slightly awkward phrasing here and there. Those can be cleaned up when you have &quot;fresh eyes&quot; in a day or so and can decide what gets the message across and what could be clearer.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
